"{\"json\": {\"addressed_weaknesses\": [\"Established an introductory regression audience and concrete learning objectives appropriate to the required teaching-note format.\", \"Connected mean-residual correction to least-squares intercept fitting with a fixed baseline.\", \"Explained why constant residuals guarantee exact agreement in the supplied construction.\", \"Reframed the error values as algebraic consequences reproduced by a deterministic calculation.\", \"Removed loosely connected related work and the mismatched Geman reference discussion.\", \"Consolidated duplicated provenance into one visible reproducibility supplement while preserving the required figures and source references.\"], \"integrated_answers\": [\"Specified that readers should learn residual centering, fixed-baseline intercept fitting, absolute versus squared error aggregation, and the interpretation of fitting-observation errors.\", \"Added a sum-of-squares decomposition establishing the mean residual as the unique least-squares offset.\", \"Clarified the relationship between the algebra, the R calculation, the table, and the unchanged plot's title.\", \"Retained only directly relevant references using author, title, venue, and year metadata supplied by the citation map.\"], \"actions_taken\": [\"Condensed the manuscript to a short technical teaching note with the supplied article headings.\", \"Preserved all supplied observations, predictions, correction settings, MAE values, and MSE values.\", \"Assigned distinct instructional roles to the residual-flow diagram and MSE plot.\", \"Removed unverified bibliography volume and page fields rather than claiming verification against unavailable original publications.\", \"Preserved the supplied figure images, exact source paths, and authoritative source checksums without reconstructing figure recipes.\", \"Removed forced page breaks and repeated provenance text.\", \"Added no experiments, empirical results, or promises of future experiments.\"]}, \"latex\": \"\\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n\\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n\\\\usepackage{graphicx}\\n\\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n\\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n\\\\title{A Finite Intercept Correction Calculation}\\n\\\\author{Anonymous}\\n\\\\date{}\\n\\n\\\\begin{document}\\n\\\\maketitle\\n\\n\\\\begin{abstract}\\nThis teaching note connects residual averaging to least-squares intercept fitting through five synthetic observations. With the baseline predictor fixed at $2x$ and targets satisfying $y_i=2x_i+3$, every baseline residual equals $3$. Their mean gives the fitted offset $b=3$. A sum-of-squares decomposition explains both this choice and the resulting exact agreement. Mean absolute and mean squared errors at three supplied offsets are algebraically determined and reproduced by a deterministic R calculation. A table and two figures support instruction in residual centering, error aggregation, and the interpretation of errors on observations used for fitting.\\n\\\\end{abstract}\\n\\n\\\\section{Introduction}\\n\\nThe intended audience is students beginning regression who understand sample means and squared errors. The educational objective is to connect three operations often introduced separately: computing residuals, fitting an intercept, and summarizing errors. Readers should be able to derive the fitted offset, explain why zero error occurs in this construction, and identify which observations determine each reported quantity.\\n\\nThe example fixes the baseline $f_0(x)=2x$ and supplies\\n\\\\[\\nx=(-2,-1,0,1,2), \\\\qquad y=(-1,1,3,5,7).\\n\\\\]\\nOnly an additive scalar $b$ is fitted. Holding the baseline fixed isolates the role of the intercept: its estimation shifts every prediction by the same amount while preserving the supplied slope. The derivation, computational flow, and error table provide complementary ways to inspect this elementary calculation.\\n\\n\\\\section{Related Work}\\n\\nResidual examination supports numerical and graphical understanding of fitted models \\\\cite{Anscombe1963ExaminationAnalysis}. Here, residuals provide the quantities from which the offset is computed. Gneiting \\\\cite{Gneiting2011MakingEvaluating} discusses matching error scores to evaluation objectives; this note makes the absolute and squared aggregations explicit. Efron \\\\cite{Efron1986HowBiased} distinguishes apparent error, calculated using fitting observations, from prediction error on future observations. That distinction determines how to interpret the table below: its entries describe agreement on the same five observations that determine the offset.\\n\\n\\\\section{Method}\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Finite Calculation and Error Definitions}\\n\\nFor $i=1,\\\\ldots,5$, define the baseline predictions and residuals by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\hat y_i^{(0)}=2x_i,\\\\qquad r_i=y_i-\\\\hat y_i^{(0)}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe supplied values give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\hat y^{(0)}=(-4,-2,0,2,4),\\\\qquad r=(3,3,3,3,3).\\n\\\\]\\nAn offset produces $\\\\hat y_i(b)=2x_i+b$ and residual\\n$e_i(b)=r_i-b$. The error summaries are\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{MAE}(b)=\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\sum_{i=1}^{5}|r_i-b|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{MSE}(b)=\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\sum_{i=1}^{5}(r_i-b)^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the data construction directly implies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{MAE}(b)=|3-b|,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\operatorname{MSE}(b)=(3-b)^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe tabulated values are evaluations of these identities at the three supplied settings.\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Mean Residual Correction and Computational Flow}\\n\\nFitting the intercept with the baseline fixed means choosing $b$ to minimize the residual sum of squares. Let\\n$\\\\bar r=\\\\frac{1}{5}\\\\sum_{i=1}^{5}r_i$. Expanding around this mean gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_{i=1}^{5}(r_i-b)^2\\n=\\n\\\\sum_{i=1}^{5}(r_i-\\\\bar r)^2\\n+5(b-\\\\bar r)^2.\\n\\\\]\\nThe cross term vanishes because $\\\\sum_i(r_i-\\\\bar r)=0$.\\nThe first term is independent of $b$, and the second is uniquely minimized at\\n\\\\[\\nb^\\\\star=\\\\bar r=\\\\frac{3+3+3+3+3}{5}=3.\\n\\\\]\\nThis establishes the mean-residual correction as the least-squares intercept for the fixed baseline.\\n\\nSubtracting the mean centers the residuals. In this construction,\\n$r_i=\\\\bar r$ at every index, so every centered residual is zero and\\n$\\\\hat y(3)=(-1,1,3,5,7)=y$. The decomposition makes the reason for exact agreement explicit: all baseline discrepancies are the same constant.\\n\\nFigure~\\\\ref{fig:residual_workflow} supports tracing dependencies. Targets enter both residual computation and error evaluation; the dashed path highlights that reuse. Students can follow the arrows to distinguish the supplied baseline from the fitted offset and the resulting predictions.\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[!htbp]\\n    \\\\centering\\n    \\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\linewidth]{figures/fig_residual_workflow.jpg}\\n    \\\\caption{Computational dependencies for the five supplied observations.\\n    Baseline residuals determine their mean $b=3$, which shifts the fixed\\n    predictions to the targets. The dashed path identifies the observations\\n    shared by intercept fitting and in-sample evaluation.}\\n    \\\\label{fig:residual_workflow}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\n\\\\section{Experiments}\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Observations and Evaluation Protocol}\\n\\nThe supplied experiment is one deterministic base R calculation. It computes the mean of $y-2x$ and evaluates offsets $b=0,1.5,3$ against all five targets. These are three settings within that calculation. Throughout, the baseline slope remains $2$.\\n\\nThe calculation reproduces quantities specified by the algebra. Its results should therefore be read as an arithmetic check and a demonstration of fitting-observation error. The observation set used to calculate each error is exactly the set used to obtain $b^\\\\star$.\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Computed Errors and Reproducible Figures}\\n\\nTable~\\\\ref{tab:recorded_errors} reproduces the supplied R results. At $b=0$, each residual is $3$. Adding $1.5$ leaves each residual equal to $1.5$, so MAE becomes $1.5$ and MSE becomes $2.25$. At $b=3$, both summaries are zero.\\n\\n\\\\begin{table}[!htbp]\\n    \\\\centering\\n    \\\\caption{Computed errors from the supplied deterministic R calculation.\\n    Every entry also follows exactly from\\n    $\\\\operatorname{MAE}(b)=|3-b|$ and\\n    $\\\\operatorname{MSE}(b)=(3-b)^2$.}\\n    \\\\label{tab:recorded_errors}\\n    \\\\begin{tabular}{rrr}\\n        \\\\toprule\\n        Added $b$ & Mean absolute error & Mean squared error \\\\\\\\\\n        \\\\midrule\\n        0   & 3   & 9    \\\\\\\\\\n        1.5 & 1.5 & 2.25 \\\\\\\\\\n        3   & 0   & 0    \\\\\\\\\\n        \\\\bottomrule\\n    \\\\end{tabular}\\n\\\\end{table}\\n\\nThe intermediate setting illustrates the difference between error aggregations: halving the common residual halves its absolute value and quarters its square. This comparison concerns the arithmetic of the two summaries on these observations.\\n\\nFigure~\\\\ref{fig:intercept_error} provides a visual reading exercise using exactly the three supplied MSE values. Its source specifies these coordinates directly. The plot and table are two presentations of the same quantities, while the derivation explains them. A useful instructional check is to calculate the intermediate point before reading its annotation and then explain the zero point using the residual decomposition.\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[!htbp]\\n    \\\\centering\\n    \\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\linewidth]{figures/fig_intercept_error.jpg}\\n    \\\\caption{The three supplied values\\n    $(b,\\\\operatorname{MSE})=(0,9),(1.5,2.25),(3,0)$.\\n    The unchanged graphic's title uses ``Measured'' for values reproduced\\n    by the deterministic calculation; all three are algebraically determined.\\n    Each point evaluates the same five observations used for fitting.}\\n    \\\\label{fig:intercept_error}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\n\\\\section{Conclusion}\\n\\nThe five-point calculation connects mean-residual correction to least-squares intercept fitting with a fixed baseline. Its constant residuals explain exact agreement at $b=3$, and its intermediate offset illustrates absolute versus squared error aggregation. The instructional outcome is a traceable account of what is supplied, what is fitted, and what the resulting in-sample errors describe.\\n\\n\\\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{Anscombe1963ExaminationAnalysis}\\nFrancis John Anscombe and John W. Tukey.\\n\\\\newblock The Examination and Analysis of Residuals.\\n\\\\newblock \\\\emph{Technometrics}, 1963.\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{Gneiting2011MakingEvaluating}\\nTilmann Gneiting.\\n\\\\newblock Making and Evaluating Point Forecasts.\\n\\\\newblock \\\\emph{Journal of the American Statistical Association}, 2011.\\n\\n\\\\bibitem{Efron1986HowBiased}\\nBradley Efron.\\n\\\\newblock How Biased is the Apparent Error Rate of a Prediction Rule?\\n\\\\newblock \\\\emph{Journal of the American Statistical Association}, 1986.\\n\\n\\\\end{thebibliography}\\n\\n% PAPER_ORCHESTRA_PROVENANCE_BEGIN\\n\\\\clearpage\\n\\\\section*{Figure source provenance}\\nThe files below are the exact sources that produced the embedded figure previews. Paths are relative to this manuscript's source directory. The accompanying \\\\texttt{figure\\\\char95{}provenance.json} records all artifact checksums.\\\\par\\n\\\\medskip\\\\noindent\\\\textbf{Figure~\\\\ref{fig:intercept_error} (\\\\texttt{fig\\\\char95{}intercept\\\\char95{}error.jpg}).}\\\\par\\n\\\\noindent R/ggplot2 source: \\\\texttt{figure\\\\char95{}code/\\\\allowbreak{}fig\\\\char95{}intercept\\\\char95{}error/\\\\allowbreak{}source.R}.\\\\par\\n\\\\noindent Source SHA256: \\\\texttt{2479e40e\\\\allowbreak{}8d39e8e1\\\\allowbreak{}6c29f883\\\\allowbreak{}7e3b8df9\\\\allowbreak{}909f9f65\\\\allowbreak{}8bb35b95\\\\allowbreak{}9fda0b12\\\\allowbreak{}96debbe4}.\\\\par\\n\\\\noindent Rendering metadata: \\\\texttt{figure\\\\char95{}code/\\\\allow